    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "`class_values` is a list of dictionaries, one per class, like `{'values': [0], 'summary': 'No Data'}`. We want two lists out of it: all the codes and all the names. This is a job for a list comprehension!\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Exercise {.unlisted}\n",
        "\n",
        "First, predict the output of each line. Then run it to check your prediction.\n",
        "\n",
        "1. `class_values[0]`\n",
        "2. `class_values[0]['values']`\n",
        "3. `class_values[0]['values'][0]`\n",
        "4. `[c['values'][0] for c in class_values]`\n",
        "\n",
        "Now write your own:\n",
        "\n",
        "5. Write a `for` loop that collects the `summary` value of each element of `class_values` into a list.\n",
        "6. Rewrite your loop from 5 as a list comprehension.\n",
        "\n",
        "<!--\n",
        "```python\n",
        "# 5. For loop\n",
        "names = []\n",
        "for c in class_values:\n",
        "    names.append(c['summary'])\n",
        "print(names)\n",
        "\n",
        "# 6. List comprehension\n",
        "names = [c['summary'] for c in class_values]\n",
        "print(names)\n",
        "```\n",
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "Now let's put both list comprehensions to work: one gets the codes and the other gets the class names, and together they make a data frame with one row per class. We'll call it `labels_from_item`, so we don't overwrite the `labels` we read from the CSV:"
